1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:11,080
As the walls of communism collapsed in 1991, the historical change would have an unexpected impact on our knowledge of the world's oceans.

2
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:24,220
The end of Cold War hostilities would open the door to a cornucopia of amazing new devices that had been developed for top secret searching and researching of 71% of

3
00:00:24,221 --> 00:00:27,600
our planet that is shrouded in mystery and water.

4
00:00:27,601 --> 00:00:32,600
The Cold War has produced this explosion of technology which is now opening the oceans.

5
00:00:32,601 --> 00:00:35,600
There's this basic, wonderful peace dividend that happens,

6
00:00:35,601 --> 00:00:40,600
where all this expertise and knowledge and equipment comes spilling into the civil sector.

7
00:00:40,601 --> 00:00:46,600
Both Soviet and American governments began allowing scientific and industrial researchers

8
00:00:46,601 --> 00:00:53,600
to use the technological arsenal that had been developed for secret military probing of the oceans.

9
00:00:53,601 --> 00:01:00,460
This would lead to the latest and most far-reaching development in mankind's quest

10
00:01:00,461 --> 00:01:07,280
to expose the sea's unexplored depths of crushing pressure and eternal darkness.

11
00:01:12,020 --> 00:01:17,840
The history of man's exploration of the oceans is as old as recorded history itself.

12
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:24,920
Relics from ancient civilizations show people diving in the sea as long as 6,000 years ago.

13
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:32,660
Somewhere in the annals of time, some inventive swimmer discovered that by breathing through a hollow reed,

14
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:39,460
you could paddle indefinitely beneath the surface of the waves, and an early version of the snorkel was born.

15
00:01:39,461 --> 00:01:46,500
legend has it that one of the first people to use an enclosed chamber to explore the sea

16
00:01:46,501 --> 00:01:54,520
was alexander the great around 350 bc he supposedly had his artisans construct a glass

17
00:01:54,521 --> 00:02:00,200
barrow large enough for him to sit in he then had it sealed and was lowered into the water

18
00:02:00,201 --> 00:02:07,840
for mankind's first look at the ocean depths it was also around the same time that alexander's

19
00:02:07,841 --> 00:02:13,920
friend and teacher, Aristotle, documented the first known use of a self-contained underwater

20
00:02:13,921 --> 00:02:16,880
breathing apparatus, or scuba for short.

21
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:25,600
Aristotle described how Greek divers searching for coral in the Mediterranean would trap

22
00:02:25,601 --> 00:02:32,160
air inside a dried animal bladder and drag it underwater with them as an added air supply

23
00:02:32,161 --> 00:02:33,100
during their work.

24
00:02:33,101 --> 00:02:41,640
Although mariners plied the seas throughout the centuries, the ocean remained a place of mystery and foreboding.

25
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:51,320
Sailors told tales of demonic monsters who inhabited the deep, and many ships were thought to have fallen off the edge of the world.

26
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:56,100
But the Renaissance brought a new age of enlightenment and discovery.

27
00:02:56,101 --> 00:03:01,300
The exploration of the sea started thousands and thousands of years ago

28
00:03:01,301 --> 00:03:05,840
as people would wander away out onto the ocean in tiny little boats and canoes

29
00:03:05,841 --> 00:03:07,600
and bigger and bigger boats.

30
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:12,520
And they'd do it for all kinds of reasons, for migration, for transporting goods,

31
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:14,440
for going out and looking for food.

32
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:18,180
And they kept going farther and farther and discovering eventually,

33
00:03:18,181 --> 00:03:24,060
in the age of discovery, that basically all the seas and oceans on the planet were one.

34
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:29,820
After Columbus landed in the New World in 1492,

35
00:03:29,821 --> 00:03:33,280
the common perception of the sea began to change.

36
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:35,840
The sea had always been a barrier.

37
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:39,040
Now it became a bridge to discovery.

38
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:46,280
This changing perception sparked new interest in inventing devices for exploring the deep.

39
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:49,500
The inventions fell into two broad categories.

40
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:53,480
The first had to do with underwater chambers and vehicles.

41
00:03:53,481 --> 00:03:58,480
The other was equipment that an individual diver might wear.

42
00:03:58,481 --> 00:04:04,480
Although none were ever built, Leonardo da Vinci designed early versions of the submarine,

43
00:04:04,481 --> 00:04:07,480
as well as swim fins and snorkels.

44
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:14,480
The invention that became the most popular and widespread, however, was the diving bell.

45
00:04:14,481 --> 00:04:22,480
Trapping air like an inverted glass, the bells were common throughout Europe and the Mediterranean by the 1600s.

46
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:30,480
They were used for such important practical jobs as repairing the hulls of ships and salvage operations.

47
00:04:30,481 --> 00:04:36,480
Study of the ocean itself progressed slowly at this time.

48
00:04:36,481 --> 00:04:41,480
Most people believed that the sea was barren in the lower depths.

49
00:04:41,481 --> 00:04:45,480
Scientists of the day called this the Azoic Theory.

50
00:04:45,481 --> 00:04:51,480
Governments had no financial incentive to launch expensive expeditions to study what was felt to be

51
00:04:51,481 --> 00:04:59,280
felt to be a vast wasteland. At the end of the 18th century, Benjamin Franklin would

52
00:04:59,281 --> 00:05:03,769
inadvertently become a founding father of oceanography.

53
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:06,380
While America was still a colony

54
00:05:06,381 --> 00:05:11,067
of England, Franklin was the deputy postmaster general.

55
00:05:11,068 --> 00:05:14,220
During a trip to London, he was asked

56
00:05:14,221 --> 00:05:20,599
by the lords of the treasury whether he could help solve a nautical riddle.

57
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:21,620
New York and

58
00:05:21,621 --> 00:05:26,320
Rhode Island were very near each other, and both were comparable distances from England.

59
00:05:27,260 --> 00:05:32,460
Yet mail packets sailing from London to New York were taking two weeks longer than ships

60
00:05:32,461 --> 00:05:34,555
sailing to Rhode Island.

61
00:05:34,556 --> 00:05:39,880
Upon returning to America, Franklin consulted his cousin, who

62
00:05:39,881 --> 00:05:41,620
was a Nantucket whaling captain.

63
00:05:42,620 --> 00:05:45,420
The future statesman was told that New England fishermen

64
00:05:45,421 --> 00:05:48,580
were well acquainted with the mighty river within the sea

65
00:05:48,581 --> 00:05:51,460
that flowed easterly across the Atlantic.

66
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:54,900
Mail ships traveling to New York were delayed

67
00:05:54,901 --> 00:05:57,620
because they were sailing against a powerful force.

68
00:05:58,660 --> 00:06:01,420
Franklin had his cousin draw up a sketch of the current

69
00:06:01,421 --> 00:06:04,680
and soon published the first map of the Gulf Stream.

70
00:06:05,380 --> 00:06:07,300
A full century would pass, however,

71
00:06:07,301 --> 00:06:09,940
before the first serious studies of the stream.

72
00:06:11,520 --> 00:06:14,140
One of the earliest consolidated efforts

73
00:06:14,141 --> 00:06:17,560
to determine the shape of an ocean basin

74
00:06:17,561 --> 00:06:21,020
was motivated by a desire to find a place

75
00:06:21,021 --> 00:06:23,240
where they could lay a telegraphic cable

76
00:06:23,241 --> 00:06:26,300
across from North America to Britain.

77
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:30,340
And they found something called the telegraphic plateau,

78
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:33,300
and that's indeed where the first telegraphic cable was laid.

79
00:06:33,301 --> 00:06:41,540
Interestingly, development of this totally unrelated invention would motivate further research

80
00:06:41,541 --> 00:06:45,460
and have a profound impact on underwater exploration.

81
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:50,900
The year after the transatlantic cable was completed,

82
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:55,100
another event occurred that would spark scientific curiosity about the sea.

83
00:06:56,460 --> 00:07:02,980
In 1859, Charles Darwin published a controversial work called The Origin of Species.

84
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:09,400
His revolutionary ideas on evolution suggested that species could be preserved for eons

85
00:07:09,401 --> 00:07:14,440
if they inhabited what he called some distant and isolated station.

86
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:21,660
Whereas the ocean depths had previously been viewed as deep, vacuous wastelands,

87
00:07:22,140 --> 00:07:27,280
Darwin's idea suggested they might possibly hold long-forgotten life forms.

88
00:07:28,420 --> 00:07:31,420
After Origin of the Species was published in 1859,

89
00:07:31,421 --> 00:07:34,280
you get a lot of people starting to say,

90
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:36,460
well, who knows what's down there?

91
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:39,000
Let's take a real hard look in terms of the creatures.

92
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:42,000
And lo and behold, they start finding living fossils

93
00:07:42,001 --> 00:07:43,680
that whetted people's appetites

94
00:07:43,681 --> 00:07:46,380
and got the scientists more and more interested in the deep sea.

95
00:07:47,020 --> 00:07:49,500
By now, both commerce and science

96
00:07:49,501 --> 00:07:51,820
were interested in the nature of the sea.

97
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:55,420
But it would take the publication of another book in 1871

98
00:07:56,100 --> 00:07:58,380
to spark the public's enthusiasm.

99
00:07:58,960 --> 00:08:02,260
Jules Verne's book, 20,000 Leagues Under the Sea,

100
00:08:02,740 --> 00:08:07,240
told of this wonderful submarine adventure around the globe,

101
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:09,020
and that captured the world's imagination.

102
00:08:09,580 --> 00:08:15,180
It was the first time anybody had presented an epic story of discovery underneath the sea like that,

103
00:08:15,340 --> 00:08:16,260
and people loved it.

104
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:22,560
Gradually, the sea's perpetual night became in the popular mind what outer space is today,

105
00:08:22,561 --> 00:08:28,920
a place to explore, a region romantic, alien, yet exciting.

106
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:31,540
Sea studies made headlines.

107
00:08:32,140 --> 00:08:35,260
Each new find became a matter of national pride.

108
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:39,960
When the United States announced plans to send its most famous naturalist,

109
00:08:40,080 --> 00:08:42,420
Louis Agassiz, on a survey cruise,

110
00:08:43,040 --> 00:08:45,940
the nation which claimed to be the mistress of the seas

111
00:08:45,941 --> 00:08:48,320
felt its leadership slipping away.

112
00:08:48,321 --> 00:08:53,100
Although the American effort proved unremarkable,

113
00:08:53,101 --> 00:09:00,300
the challenge would lead Great Britain to announce the world's first oceanographic expedition to circle the globe.

114
00:09:01,340 --> 00:09:05,640
The vessel would be christened appropriately enough the HMS Challenger.

115
00:09:06,420 --> 00:09:10,400
The first American submarine, the Turtle, was propelled by hand cranks.

116
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:14,320
It was built by a Yale student with guidance from Benjamin Franklin

117
00:09:14,321 --> 00:09:17,740
and used with little success in the American Revolution.

118
00:09:18,520 --> 00:09:21,840
deep sea exploration will continue on Modern Marvels.

119
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:29,420
The morning of December 21st, 1872, launched the modern epic of oceanographic exploration.

120
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:35,120
That's the day the British warship Challenger set sail from Portsmouth, England,

121
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:38,760
on a three-and-a-half-year expedition around the world.

122
00:09:40,900 --> 00:09:44,580
Its mission was to study every aspect of the sea,

123
00:09:44,581 --> 00:09:49,880
from life forms in the deep to the shape of the bottom to currents and chemistry.

124
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:56,100
Leading the expedition was renowned naturalist Sir Charles Wayville Thompson.

125
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:08,297
One of the most important discoveries of the expedition occurred after the scientists sent a dredge down to scoop mud from the bottom of one of the deepest spots in

126
00:10:08,298 --> 00:10:08,998
the ocean.

127
00:10:10,760 --> 00:10:14,080
Down goes the dredge, up it comes hours and hours later.

128
00:10:14,081 --> 00:10:18,580
they found these really ugly tubes,

129
00:10:18,880 --> 00:10:24,080
and they tapped one open, and there was this ugly, limp, gray worm.

130
00:10:24,900 --> 00:10:29,380
And a cheer went up, because they realized that life existed

131
00:10:29,381 --> 00:10:32,120
all the way down in the deepest reaches of the ocean,

132
00:10:32,580 --> 00:10:34,940
and the Azoic theory was gone.

133
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:41,920
During the arduous trip, Thompson and his 240 fellow scientists and crewmen

134
00:10:41,921 --> 00:10:44,800
discovered almost 5,000 new species,

135
00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:49,220
charted about 140 square miles of ocean bottom,

136
00:10:49,860 --> 00:10:51,960
collected over 1,000 water samples,

137
00:10:52,440 --> 00:10:55,140
and sounded the depths of every ocean but the Arctic.

138
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,140
At the end of three and a half years,

139
00:10:59,900 --> 00:11:01,940
the Challenger returned back to Britain,

140
00:11:01,941 --> 00:11:06,200
and they thought that they probably had enough material

141
00:11:06,201 --> 00:11:10,900
to keep the men busy for about five years writing a report.

142
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:18,000
It turned out that it took 20 years and that the report was 50 volumes.

143
00:11:18,920 --> 00:11:24,480
The Challenger report provided a framework for the new science of oceanography

144
00:11:24,481 --> 00:11:28,400
and helped to stimulate further research throughout the world.

145
00:11:30,140 --> 00:11:35,620
One of the major innovations in oceanography would be developed because of a tragedy at sea.

146
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:41,660
In 1912, the world's most famous ship, the Titanic, sank on its maiden voyage

147
00:11:41,661 --> 00:11:43,220
after hitting an iceberg.

148
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:47,780
More than 1,500 people drowned.

149
00:11:50,860 --> 00:11:54,340
One man determined that he would do whatever he could

150
00:11:54,341 --> 00:11:57,320
to keep a similar disaster from ever happening again.

151
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:02,880
Reginald Fessenden, a former assistant of Thomas Edison's,

152
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:06,140
had been tinkering with a device that located objects

153
00:12:06,141 --> 00:12:07,940
by bouncing sound off them.

154
00:12:08,600 --> 00:12:10,240
He intensified his research,

155
00:12:10,241 --> 00:12:14,160
and by 1914 had developed an early form of sonar.

156
00:12:16,020 --> 00:12:20,960
Sonar would eventually become one of the most important tools in oceanographic study.

157
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:25,740
When World War I began several years later,

158
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:30,860
Fessenden modified his invention and it became an invaluable part of Allied defense,

159
00:12:31,400 --> 00:12:33,300
helping to locate enemy submarines.

160
00:12:35,120 --> 00:12:39,120
The U.S. Navy had purchased its first sub in 1898,

161
00:12:39,121 --> 00:12:44,160
almost 300 years after the demonstration of a functioning submarine in England.

162
00:12:45,160 --> 00:12:48,540
During the war, subs became an accepted part of the fleet

163
00:12:48,541 --> 00:12:51,540
and proved to be one of its most effective weapons.

164
00:12:54,600 --> 00:12:59,140
What had limited the downward range of human divers was water pressure.

165
00:12:59,680 --> 00:13:01,800
This is the effective pressure in the deep sea.

166
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:04,300
This is a regular styrofoam cup.

167
00:13:04,301 --> 00:13:06,940
I did a dive down a mile and a half in the ocean,

168
00:13:06,941 --> 00:13:09,340
and I put one of these on the outside.

169
00:13:09,700 --> 00:13:12,600
The pressure is very uniform, as you can see on the cup.

170
00:13:13,720 --> 00:13:18,420
Anything that has a cavity or space inside of it collapses.

171
00:13:19,300 --> 00:13:22,140
Metal objects will collapse unless they're made out of very thick metal

172
00:13:22,141 --> 00:13:25,560
and unless there's perfect uniformity about it.

173
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,520
The most uniform kind of metal object is a sphere,

174
00:13:28,720 --> 00:13:31,800
so a lot of the submersibles that go down in the deep sea are spherical.

175
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:36,640
More than 20 years after the end of the First World War,

176
00:13:36,641 --> 00:13:42,360
two daring explorers introduced the device which would revolutionize the study of the deep ocean.

177
00:13:42,900 --> 00:13:49,380
Otis Barton used his own money to build a sphere to be hung on the end of a cable.

178
00:13:50,280 --> 00:13:56,480
It would have some power coming down for lights and some hard wire for communications.

179
00:13:57,360 --> 00:14:03,780
He selected William Beebe to go down with him because of his interest in deep sea creatures.

180
00:14:03,781 --> 00:14:12,780
Between 1930 and 1934, Beebe and Barton made a series of courageous plunges in the sphere.

181
00:14:12,781 --> 00:14:18,780
They sealed themselves inside this and they were lowered on a cable down into the deep sea.

182
00:14:18,781 --> 00:14:24,780
And they were the first ones to see with their own eyes this world.

183
00:14:24,781 --> 00:14:32,780
This world of pitch darkness that people only had really vague ideas about its composition.

184
00:14:32,781 --> 00:14:39,780
Every dive brought new discoveries and amazing new creatures.

185
00:14:39,781 --> 00:14:47,780
Scientists had to radically alter their previous notions as the lower depths were found to be teeming with life.

186
00:14:47,781 --> 00:14:52,780
One of the more fantastic discoveries was luminescence.

187
00:14:52,781 --> 00:14:59,780
Many creatures in the ocean's inky depths produced their own light and glowed in the dark.

188
00:14:59,781 --> 00:15:04,220
Hardhat divers of the day could dive to a few hundred feet.

189
00:15:04,221 --> 00:15:09,740
Submarines could travel slightly deeper. The bathysphere in contrast succeeded in

190
00:15:09,741 --> 00:15:11,700
descending more than half a mile

191
00:15:11,701 --> 00:15:15,820
into the deep.

192
00:15:15,821 --> 00:15:20,540
These two fearless explorers changed scientific concepts about the sea

193
00:15:20,541 --> 00:15:23,900
and opened the door to the oceans lower depths.

194
00:15:23,901 --> 00:15:28,580
Throughout the beginnings of the 20th century

195
00:15:28,581 --> 00:15:32,740
and during World War II, sonar devices and techniques were refined.

196
00:15:33,700 --> 00:15:37,700
After the war, two scientists, Marie Tharp and Bruce Heason,

197
00:15:38,180 --> 00:15:42,760
gathered all the new-sounding data that had been collected by scientists around the world

198
00:15:42,761 --> 00:15:46,520
and used it to devise a map of the ocean floor.

199
00:15:47,880 --> 00:15:51,040
The map that resulted changed our vision of the planet.

200
00:15:51,041 --> 00:15:56,520
It showed that a single underwater mountain range runs through the basins of the world's oceans.

201
00:15:58,580 --> 00:16:03,120
Discovery of the 46,000-mile ridge protruding from the Earth's crust

202
00:16:03,121 --> 00:16:06,580
and subsequent studies of the new map would aid in the development

203
00:16:06,581 --> 00:16:10,840
of a revolutionary new theory called plate tectonics.

204
00:16:11,940 --> 00:16:17,340
The outermost surface of the Earth is fairly rigid material.

205
00:16:17,341 --> 00:16:18,840
It's fairly brittle.

206
00:16:19,820 --> 00:16:24,900
And for reasons that we're still trying to figure out,

207
00:16:24,901 --> 00:16:31,620
this brittle surface is broken up into some very large pieces

208
00:16:31,621 --> 00:16:33,880
that move around relative to one another.

209
00:16:34,760 --> 00:16:39,560
This plate tectonic theory held that where these plates rub together,

210
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:42,380
geological activity occurs.

211
00:16:43,420 --> 00:16:47,820
At some points, new mountain ranges can be pushed up through the Earth's crust.

212
00:16:49,480 --> 00:16:50,780
Earthquakes can occur.

213
00:16:50,781 --> 00:16:55,140
or the superheated interior can be exposed.

214
00:16:57,180 --> 00:17:02,420
Within the next few years, scientific study of the new findings would be revolutionized.

215
00:17:02,940 --> 00:17:05,740
An amazing new submersible would soon be introduced

216
00:17:05,741 --> 00:17:09,380
that would allow exploration of Earth's final frontier.

217
00:17:10,460 --> 00:17:14,820
The Gulf Stream transports 2.5 billion cubic feet of water per second.

218
00:17:15,340 --> 00:17:18,620
That's 3,000 times the volume of the Mississippi River.

219
00:17:19,380 --> 00:17:21,120
Modern marvels will be right back.

220
00:17:21,800 --> 00:17:27,120
Man's adventure into the deepest reaches of the ocean began in August of 1953.

221
00:17:28,020 --> 00:17:30,546
That's when the Swiss scientist Dr.

222
00:17:30,547 --> 00:17:34,660
Auguste Piccard devised his bathyscaf, named the Trieste.

223
00:17:35,340 --> 00:17:41,260
Meaning deep boat, the bathyscaf was capable of diving to some of the deepest parts of the ocean.

224
00:17:41,261 --> 00:17:51,280
the Trieste was a breakthrough because unlike earlier submersibles it wasn't connected to a

225
00:17:51,281 --> 00:17:59,260
surface ship and at full mobility to roam and explore the oceans depths and even more impressive

226
00:17:59,261 --> 00:18:05,800
the new vessel was designed to attempt depths never dreamed possible before the Trieste can

227
00:18:05,801 --> 00:18:13,117
go roughly a hundred times deeper than any diver has ever gone or any submarine.

228
00:18:13,118 --> 00:18:14,580
This was a major

229
00:18:14,581 --> 00:18:21,820
technical achievement to be able to go down where the pressures are extreme, the environment is

230
00:18:21,821 --> 00:18:30,600
really hostile to man being there, and with the Trieste, they had the mechanism to get there and

231
00:18:30,601 --> 00:18:31,301
back.

232
00:18:31,302 --> 00:18:38,140
The Trieste had two main structural elements, a long horizontal buoyancy chamber

233
00:18:38,141 --> 00:18:44,200
and a spherical observation chamber attached underneath, which held the two-person crew.

234
00:18:45,320 --> 00:18:47,954
The U.S. Navy was so impressed with Dr.

235
00:18:47,955 --> 00:18:51,060
Picard's vessel that they purchased it in 1958

236
00:18:51,061 --> 00:18:54,404
and began remodeling and improving its design.

237
00:18:54,405 --> 00:18:57,820
With its reinforced walls, the improved Trieste

238
00:18:57,821 --> 00:19:01,220
would attempt its greatest undersea accomplishment two years later.

239
00:19:01,760 --> 00:19:05,120
The ultimate danger, of course, is complete collapse of the sphere.

240
00:19:05,121 --> 00:19:09,780
It's like breaking a Christmas tree ornament.

241
00:19:10,180 --> 00:19:12,120
It's all over instantaneously.

242
00:19:13,240 --> 00:19:16,420
On the morning of January 23, 1960,

243
00:19:17,060 --> 00:19:22,020
the Trieste prepared to descend seven miles to the deepest spot in the sea.

244
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:26,200
The seas were running eight to twelve feet high,

245
00:19:26,201 --> 00:19:29,940
and it was marginal for taking a craft down.

246
00:19:31,160 --> 00:19:34,500
The two individuals that went down on the deepest dive

247
00:19:34,501 --> 00:19:37,920
were Lieutenant Walsh and Jacques Piccard,

248
00:19:38,120 --> 00:19:40,280
son of the inventor Auguste Piccard.

249
00:19:41,540 --> 00:19:46,180
The speed of the bathyscap in a dive or ascent

250
00:19:46,181 --> 00:19:49,060
is about one mile per hour

251
00:19:49,061 --> 00:19:51,900
or about the same speed that you would feel in an elevator.

252
00:19:52,760 --> 00:19:55,900
The Trieste fell through the undersea darkness

253
00:19:55,901 --> 00:19:57,700
for more than four hours.

254
00:19:58,760 --> 00:20:01,900
The target at the bottom was three miles long

255
00:20:01,901 --> 00:20:03,600
and a half a mile wide.

256
00:20:03,860 --> 00:20:06,160
That's pretty small when you're seven miles above.

257
00:20:07,160 --> 00:20:10,060
But fortunately, it hit right on target,

258
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:15,300
and we heard them on our underwater acoustic telephone

259
00:20:15,301 --> 00:20:18,420
say that they were at bottom of the trench.

260
00:20:21,120 --> 00:20:22,960
Picard and his pilot looked out

261
00:20:22,961 --> 00:20:25,340
and were able to answer for the first time

262
00:20:25,341 --> 00:20:28,180
a question biologists had asked for years.

263
00:20:29,540 --> 00:20:36,800
Life at seven miles down, other than bacteria, was really an unknown.

264
00:20:38,120 --> 00:20:42,220
And it was fortuitous when the two people reached the bottom,

265
00:20:42,600 --> 00:20:45,560
they saw a fish, a shrimp, and a jellyfish.

266
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:50,400
Well, that very fact is indicative that life can exist anywhere in the deep ocean

267
00:20:50,401 --> 00:20:52,260
as far as depth is concerned.

268
00:20:52,261 --> 00:21:00,860
Almost nine hours after it began its dive, the Trieste surfaced, having set a depth record that has never been duplicated.

269
00:21:05,420 --> 00:21:16,140
Despite these impressive accomplishments, in 1961 the Navy decided to halt its support of deep sea research and retire the Trieste.

270
00:21:16,141 --> 00:21:25,320
It would take another tragedy to once again convince the government of the importance of deep sea research.

271
00:21:26,860 --> 00:21:33,600
In April 1963, America experienced its worst peacetime submarine disaster.

272
00:21:37,040 --> 00:21:37,740
The U.S.

273
00:21:37,741 --> 00:21:43,760
Navy's atomic submarine Thresher was on a shakedown cruise after leaving the yard.

274
00:21:43,761 --> 00:21:51,360
a weld broke, allowing the ship to flood, sending it past its crush depth.

275
00:21:51,860 --> 00:21:56,140
The ship imploded and caused a debris field well over a square mile.

276
00:21:56,780 --> 00:22:00,100
Although the vessel had not yet been fitted with nuclear warheads,

277
00:22:00,600 --> 00:22:05,100
its radioactive operating reactor was lying somewhere at the bottom of the sea.

278
00:22:08,720 --> 00:22:12,940
Compounding the loss of 129 lives was the fact that the stricken vessel

279
00:22:12,941 --> 00:22:14,375
could not be located.

280
00:22:14,376 --> 00:22:18,680
After several months of fruitless searching, the Navy turned to

281
00:22:18,681 --> 00:22:20,456
the mothballed Trieste.

282
00:22:20,457 --> 00:22:25,320
It dove to a depth of two miles, and there, on the ocean floor,

283
00:22:25,321 --> 00:22:28,080
was strewn the wreckage of the thresher.

284
00:22:33,980 --> 00:22:39,660
It was a major triumph for the vehicle that the Navy had abandoned, and showed the important

285
00:22:39,661 --> 00:22:45,380
practical benefits of further oceanographic development.

286
00:22:45,381 --> 00:22:50,940
Beyond the human tragedy, the Thresher incident was a political blow to the United States.

287
00:22:50,941 --> 00:22:56,620
The loss of its most advanced sub was seen as a major setback in the Cold War against

288
00:22:56,621 --> 00:23:02,920
communism and America's global effort to project strength and promote alliances.

289
00:23:02,921 --> 00:23:08,300
That episode became a watershed in the history of the Navy's interest in the deep sea.

290
00:23:08,301 --> 00:23:09,960
This became the Pearl Harbor of the deep.

291
00:23:10,380 --> 00:23:12,880
They were never going to suffer another Pearl Harbor again.

292
00:23:13,820 --> 00:23:17,420
And at that point, the fighting Navy got interested.

293
00:23:18,120 --> 00:23:22,940
And they sank billions and billions of dollars into mastering the deep.

294
00:23:23,660 --> 00:23:26,600
This commitment did not go unnoticed by the Russians,

295
00:23:26,900 --> 00:23:29,760
who made a similar decision to keep up with the Americans.

296
00:23:31,900 --> 00:23:36,680
The resulting contest between the superpowers saw enormous progress made

297
00:23:36,681 --> 00:23:43,680
in the development of military equipment and a dazzling array of underwater lasers, submersibles,

298
00:23:44,040 --> 00:23:48,886
robots and advanced sonar for investigating the world's oceans.

299
00:23:48,887 --> 00:23:51,040
One example was the American

300
00:23:51,740 --> 00:23:53,318
mini-sub named Alvin.

301
00:23:53,319 --> 00:23:58,580
Destined to become the superstar of oceanography, the short snub-nosed

302
00:23:58,581 --> 00:24:00,920
mini didn't look the part.

303
00:24:00,921 --> 00:24:06,500
Not much bigger than a minivan, the sub was able to hold three

304
00:24:06,501 --> 00:24:09,685
people and dive to a depth of 6,000 feet.

305
00:24:09,686 --> 00:24:13,180
It was designed to carry out any kind of deep

306
00:24:13,181 --> 00:24:18,260
sea research project and would be instrumental in groundbreaking discoveries for decades

307
00:24:18,261 --> 00:24:26,950
to come. The era also saw major strides in the civilian scientific community.

308
00:24:26,951 --> 00:24:27,740
Jacques

309
00:24:27,741 --> 00:24:32,780
Cousteau, inventor of modern scuba gear, built an underwater habitat in the Mediterranean,

310
00:24:33,300 --> 00:24:36,500
where two aquanauts successfully lived for a week.

311
00:24:37,640 --> 00:24:41,580
He was also instrumental in the early development of manned submersibles.

312
00:24:43,200 --> 00:24:47,820
As an explorer, innovator, and vocal proponent of deep-sea research,

313
00:24:48,580 --> 00:24:51,760
Jacques Cousteau would educate and fascinate generations.

314
00:24:51,761 --> 00:25:00,760
By far the largest most comprehensive scientific endeavor of the era began in 1957.

315
00:25:00,761 --> 00:25:09,760
July 1st marks the beginning of one of the great scientific adventures of our time, the International Geophysical Year.

316
00:25:09,761 --> 00:25:16,200
During this period, the scientists of the United States will join their efforts with

317
00:25:16,201 --> 00:25:23,320
those of the scientists of some 60 other nations to make the most intensive study ever undertaken

318
00:25:23,321 --> 00:25:25,940
of our planet.

319
00:25:25,941 --> 00:25:30,400
Governments from around the world coordinated their scientists to conduct not only studies

320
00:25:30,401 --> 00:25:37,180
of the ocean, but also of the solar system, weather, cosmic rays, atmosphere, and magnetic

321
00:25:37,181 --> 00:25:38,180
fields.

322
00:25:38,181 --> 00:25:47,480
Information gathered from this international effort would impact every area of scientific endeavor for decades to come.

323
00:25:47,680 --> 00:25:57,880
As the International Geophysical Year unfolded, another event occurred that would ultimately revolutionize the study of the world's oceans.

324
00:25:59,500 --> 00:26:03,960
In some parts of the ocean, the mud on the bottom is two miles thick.

325
00:26:04,680 --> 00:26:06,480
Modern Marvels will be right back.

326
00:26:08,180 --> 00:26:14,180
The space age had begun with the launch of the world's first satellite in 1957.

327
00:26:14,181 --> 00:26:23,680
As new data was beamed back to Earth, it didn't take long before oceanographers were wondering about the possibilities for their field as well.

328
00:26:23,681 --> 00:26:30,520
Developing and testing instruments to make the needed measurements, finding appropriate vehicles for each one,

329
00:26:30,521 --> 00:26:37,520
and obtaining financial support would consume project oceanographers for much of the next decade.

330
00:26:39,620 --> 00:26:46,620
Finally, the national program came together with the unveiling of a satellite in June 1978.

331
00:26:48,020 --> 00:26:51,320
The satellite that in a sense really cracked the door open for oceanographers

332
00:26:51,321 --> 00:26:57,520
and gave them a first really powerful taste of what oceanography from orbit might be was CSAT.

333
00:26:57,521 --> 00:27:02,520
CSAT had a short lifespan but was a major triumph.

334
00:27:02,521 --> 00:27:09,520
The satellite shut down because of a massive electrical short circuit after only 91 days of operation.

335
00:27:09,521 --> 00:27:13,520
But it produced marvelous new information about the ocean.

336
00:27:13,521 --> 00:27:18,520
The deluge of data overwhelmed many scientists, however.

337
00:27:18,521 --> 00:27:26,520
Years later, some were still struggling to analyze the information obtained by the three-month-long experiment.

338
00:27:27,520 --> 00:27:32,160
The data from spacecraft continues to change simplistic concepts of the oceans.

339
00:27:33,040 --> 00:27:38,080
Now that we have satellites, we can begin to see a half of the Earth at one time.

340
00:27:38,640 --> 00:27:41,460
There are devices now that can measure the temperature of the ocean,

341
00:27:41,760 --> 00:27:46,060
measure ice formations and their movements in the ocean,

342
00:27:46,580 --> 00:27:51,100
and also even to measure gravity to the degree that you can recreate

343
00:27:51,101 --> 00:27:55,100
the topography of the bottom of the ocean just from the satellites.

344
00:27:55,600 --> 00:27:57,320
Those are amazing accomplishments.

345
00:27:57,520 --> 00:28:10,200
Near the time of the CSAT launch, another discovery occurred that would change oceanographic history books.

346
00:28:11,420 --> 00:28:18,120
In 1977, scientists took the Alvin mini-sub to a deep ridge off the Galapagos Islands

347
00:28:18,121 --> 00:28:21,440
to research questions about plate tectonics.

348
00:28:22,220 --> 00:28:26,100
The ridge was one of the places where the Earth's plates were separating,

349
00:28:26,101 --> 00:28:28,820
exposing the superheated upper mantle.

350
00:28:30,040 --> 00:28:32,840
Scientists were puzzled because water temperature in the area

351
00:28:32,841 --> 00:28:34,940
was not uniform as had been expected

352
00:28:34,941 --> 00:28:37,280
and varied from cold to warm.

353
00:28:37,940 --> 00:28:41,580
At first, the scientists and the Alvin saw nothing but a vast wasteland

354
00:28:41,581 --> 00:28:43,060
of aging lava fields.

355
00:28:43,680 --> 00:28:46,460
Then they noticed a shimmering in the ocean's depths

356
00:28:46,461 --> 00:28:48,720
like air above a hot fire.

357
00:28:50,320 --> 00:28:54,040
When they investigated, they found fields of mineral towers

358
00:28:54,041 --> 00:29:00,560
some ten stories tall, and the towers were spewing superheated mineral-rich geysers.

359
00:29:00,880 --> 00:29:08,300
Approaching the vents, there were these large inverted stalactites, some of them 30 meters high,

360
00:29:08,960 --> 00:29:12,300
and they looked like giant termite mounds like you see in Africa.

361
00:29:12,980 --> 00:29:18,820
We started to climb up one, and it was spewing out this absolutely black-looking smoke,

362
00:29:18,821 --> 00:29:22,120
and you know you're in water. How can there be smoke in water?

363
00:29:22,400 --> 00:29:23,900
But the effect was black smoke.

364
00:29:24,040 --> 00:29:32,500
The reason the ocean temperature was not consistent, it was discovered, was that some areas were literally letting off steam.

365
00:29:33,160 --> 00:29:36,920
The surface crust was spreading to reveal the planet's superheated interior.

366
00:29:37,720 --> 00:29:41,400
This was the final piece that would solve the plate tectonic puzzle.

367
00:29:41,401 --> 00:29:52,820
The whole initial series of dives confirming the existence of hot water vents on that crust that's two miles beneath the ocean

368
00:29:52,821 --> 00:30:01,280
gave the geologists the proof they needed to explain things like continental drift, the formation of the Hawaiian islands.

369
00:30:02,120 --> 00:30:06,920
It was just like the scientists had predicted. Everything started to fit together.

370
00:30:06,921 --> 00:30:12,680
and all the voices that held off that this was poppycock fell silent.

371
00:30:13,120 --> 00:30:16,560
Then the scientists found something even more amazing.

372
00:30:17,320 --> 00:30:23,740
They found an oasis of new life forms living within waters that were hot enough to melt tin,

373
00:30:25,800 --> 00:30:31,320
tube worms 16 feet long, and crabs as big as tires.

374
00:30:31,321 --> 00:30:37,640
The discovery of the vents was probably one of the great finds in geology.

375
00:30:38,540 --> 00:30:45,800
As just a little added extra, finding the animal colonies was probably the biggest bonus of all.

376
00:30:46,180 --> 00:30:51,300
It was discovered that the smoke-like discoloration from the superheated geysers

377
00:30:51,301 --> 00:30:57,700
was microscopic bacteria that fed on the minerals being spewed from the Earth's inner core.

378
00:30:57,701 --> 00:31:03,320
Some scientists theorized that the secret of life had been discovered,

379
00:31:03,321 --> 00:31:07,520
that this was how life on Earth had begun.

380
00:31:07,521 --> 00:31:15,240
Carl Sagan always predicted that when we found the genesis of man,

381
00:31:15,241 --> 00:31:17,120
it would come from the sea.

382
00:31:18,020 --> 00:31:23,100
Here was a whole life form that didn't exist on photosynthesis

383
00:31:23,101 --> 00:31:25,840
such as we know it on the surface of the planet.

384
00:31:25,841 --> 00:31:32,160
It exists on chemosynthesis, starting with the raw materials of the planet,

385
00:31:32,660 --> 00:31:38,560
elevating into bacteria, becoming the bottom of the food chain for other forms of life.

386
00:31:39,360 --> 00:31:43,700
The little Alvin and her crew confirmed a revolutionary theory

387
00:31:43,701 --> 00:31:47,720
and revealed a new world and a new way of viewing life.

388
00:31:47,721 --> 00:31:57,620
a few years later the Alvin was instrumental in an underwater discovery that captivated people

389
00:31:57,621 --> 00:32:05,540
around the world after almost 75 years at the bottom of the sea the remains of a famous tragedy

390
00:32:05,541 --> 00:32:11,660
were found in the cold reaches of the North Atlantic the ocean at that depth two and a half

391
00:32:11,661 --> 00:32:17,239
miles is totally black. You only can see what you illuminate from the submersible.

392
00:32:17,240 --> 00:32:18,260
And as you come

393
00:32:18,261 --> 00:32:25,480
up on this gigantic vessel two and a half miles beneath the sea, the lights start to create forms

394
00:32:25,481 --> 00:32:31,052
and patterns ahead of you. And suddenly you start to pick out edges and shapes.

395
00:32:31,053 --> 00:32:32,180
And there it is,

396
00:32:32,340 --> 00:32:37,140
totally revealed in the lights of your submersible, the bow of the most famous shipwreck in the world,

397
00:32:37,141 --> 00:32:40,520
the RMS Titanic.

398
00:32:40,521 --> 00:32:46,980
Extreme cold and depths had helped to preserve the liner and the remnants of

399
00:32:46,981 --> 00:32:49,580
one of the worst disasters of the century

400
00:32:49,581 --> 00:32:56,140
now seemed almost beautiful and tranquil.

401
00:32:56,141 --> 00:32:59,600
The search for the Titanic was not an act of archaeology

402
00:32:59,601 --> 00:33:05,100
but arose from a collective fascination while leaders of the original expedition

403
00:33:05,101 --> 00:33:08,194
were adamant that the wreckage be left undisturbed.

404
00:33:08,195 --> 00:33:10,500
Many items have already been salvaged.

405
00:33:13,580 --> 00:33:19,280
The haunting saga of the Titanic still awaits its final chapter.

406
00:33:23,780 --> 00:33:28,780
When the enormous mineral deposits spewed from hydrothermal vents were discovered,

407
00:33:29,160 --> 00:33:32,197
it was not only the scientists who were excited.

408
00:33:32,198 --> 00:33:34,980
Industrialists were thrilled when they heard

409
00:33:34,981 --> 00:33:43,920
of vast new fields of minerals such as zinc, copper, silver and gold.

410
00:33:43,921 --> 00:33:49,060
The era of the oil embargo of the late 70s had driven many American oil companies to

411
00:33:49,061 --> 00:33:52,620
expand their offshore explorations.

412
00:33:52,621 --> 00:33:57,700
Development of some kinds of undersea equipment had been increased, including robots for underwater

413
00:33:57,701 --> 00:33:59,820
mining.

414
00:33:59,821 --> 00:34:05,240
No one had yet begun to exploit the vast tracts of ore-rich nodules that had been discovered

415
00:34:05,241 --> 00:34:10,900
during the International Geophysical Year expeditions several decades earlier.

416
00:34:10,901 --> 00:34:16,880
The first interest in undersea mining exploded in the 70s when people started trying to pick

417
00:34:16,881 --> 00:34:22,220
up these manganese nodules that are out there on the sea floor and have concentrations of

418
00:34:22,221 --> 00:34:26,060
cobalt and nickel and all these valuable metals in them.

419
00:34:26,061 --> 00:34:31,600
Unknown thousands of square miles of the ocean floor were covered with the round-shaped nodules

420
00:34:31,601 --> 00:34:37,540
that were in theory worth untold trillions of dollars.

421
00:34:37,541 --> 00:34:42,500
All these factors combined to start a frenzy of deep sea mining ventures during the 70s

422
00:34:42,501 --> 00:34:46,380
and 80s.

423
00:34:46,381 --> 00:34:51,920
Companies and countries began laying claim to enormous portions of the ocean floors.

424
00:34:51,921 --> 00:34:59,320
And who owns the oceans, and who should benefit from what is mined from the oceans?

425
00:34:59,321 --> 00:35:04,240
These are questions that were taken up by the United Nations in 1974.

426
00:35:04,241 --> 00:35:11,220
The UN set up rules known as Exclusive Economic Zones, or EEZs, that only caused more confusion

427
00:35:11,221 --> 00:35:12,660
and conflict.

428
00:35:12,661 --> 00:35:19,240
They allowed countries to lay claim to offshore areas that extended 200 miles from their coasts.

429
00:35:19,241 --> 00:35:25,300
Laws of geometry meant that the 200-mile area often translated into vast territories.

430
00:35:26,680 --> 00:35:33,180
For example, the tiny country known as the Cook Islands has a landmass of about 90 square miles.

431
00:35:34,000 --> 00:35:39,020
By claiming an EEZ, it gained control over an area the size of Argentina.

432
00:35:40,000 --> 00:35:46,560
Problems also arose because many countries' EEZs overlapped those claimed by other countries.

433
00:35:46,561 --> 00:35:51,560
After much wild development and hundreds of millions of dollars had been spent,

434
00:35:51,561 --> 00:35:58,560
it became apparent that at least for now, mining the ocean floors is not yet financially realistic.

435
00:35:58,561 --> 00:36:08,560
I think that at this stage in the game, enough is known of the technology that if these elements are in short supply for some reason,

436
00:36:08,561 --> 00:36:15,700
that we know how to build the gear to go out and mine them.

437
00:36:15,701 --> 00:36:23,340
It's an expensive business, so you have to set it in contrast to generating the same materials out of the land.

438
00:36:23,760 --> 00:36:30,600
But the stuff is out there, and all you have to do is go get it, but you have to bring money.

439
00:36:31,800 --> 00:36:35,820
These financial obstacles proved to be beneficial for the seas.

440
00:36:35,821 --> 00:36:42,220
Research eventually showed that some forms of scoop and dredge mining could be an ecological disaster,

441
00:36:42,221 --> 00:36:45,860
laying waste to enormous areas of the ocean floor.

442
00:36:45,861 --> 00:36:49,920
But these are all questions that await us in the future.

443
00:36:49,921 --> 00:36:53,160
Vast wealth still lies at the bottom of the sea,

444
00:36:53,161 --> 00:36:57,320
and more and better mining technologies are being developed every day.

445
00:36:59,320 --> 00:37:05,360
But another event that would have a profound impact on the future of oceanography was simmering,

446
00:37:05,361 --> 00:37:08,940
and would soon explode into headlines around the world.

447
00:37:10,700 --> 00:37:12,680
September 1991.

448
00:37:13,620 --> 00:37:16,540
The world witnessed the fall of the Communist Empire.

449
00:37:18,380 --> 00:37:21,520
Amidst crumbling worlds and political upheaval, however,

450
00:37:21,521 --> 00:37:26,600
the end of Cold War hostilities would lead to something totally unexpected.

451
00:37:28,680 --> 00:37:32,040
Out of the ashes of the global military complex

452
00:37:32,041 --> 00:37:36,320
would arise the beginning of an amazing new age of discovery

453
00:37:36,321 --> 00:37:39,580
and innovation in the exploration of the sea.

454
00:37:40,760 --> 00:37:44,460
Starting about 1993, you get this wonderful peace dividend.

455
00:37:45,220 --> 00:37:50,380
There's a wave of military technology left over from the end of the Cold War

456
00:37:50,381 --> 00:37:54,220
that is opening up the oceans like they've never been opened before.

457
00:37:55,060 --> 00:38:00,020
Reduced military budgets in both superpowers and a shattered economy in Russia

458
00:38:00,021 --> 00:38:06,100
meant that neither had the overhead to support the major technological innovations and equipment

459
00:38:06,101 --> 00:38:08,820
that had been developed during the Cold War years.

460
00:38:10,260 --> 00:38:13,420
Ways needed to be found to make them self-supporting.

461
00:38:14,540 --> 00:38:18,460
In a modern version of beating swords into plowshares,

462
00:38:18,461 --> 00:38:23,960
the superpowers through opened the doors to a wonderland of technology.

463
00:38:23,961 --> 00:38:34,040
Soviet nuclear submarines could now be rented for scientific expeditions under the ice caps

464
00:38:34,041 --> 00:38:41,400
cameras developed for night warfare could now be used to study the physical movements of dolphins

465
00:38:41,401 --> 00:38:47,400
and new uses were found for the top secret submarine tracking project called

466
00:38:47,401 --> 00:38:49,320
the sound surveillance system.

467
00:38:50,680 --> 00:38:53,860
The government spent upwards of $16 billion

468
00:38:53,861 --> 00:38:56,420
to develop this system of undersea microphones

469
00:38:56,421 --> 00:38:57,880
for tracking the Soviets.

470
00:38:58,420 --> 00:39:01,220
And after the Cold War's over, what are they going to do with it?

471
00:39:01,580 --> 00:39:03,220
They really don't have any use anymore.

472
00:39:03,400 --> 00:39:05,220
Most Soviet submarines are in port.

473
00:39:06,240 --> 00:39:09,880
The ocean and society became the beneficiaries

474
00:39:09,881 --> 00:39:12,640
of this obsolete tool of war.

475
00:39:13,240 --> 00:39:16,860
That set of stations has become a nice tool

476
00:39:16,861 --> 00:39:18,780
for scientific work.

477
00:39:19,140 --> 00:39:21,980
Seismologists have found it to be an excellent way

478
00:39:21,981 --> 00:39:23,680
of listening to earthquakes

479
00:39:23,681 --> 00:39:26,100
and to submarine volcanic explosions.

480
00:39:26,520 --> 00:39:29,680
It's far more sensitive than any other methods.

481
00:39:30,620 --> 00:39:33,780
And the biologists who study the behavior of marine mammals

482
00:39:33,781 --> 00:39:36,620
have found it extraordinarily useful

483
00:39:36,621 --> 00:39:39,660
to learn about whales and other marine mammals.

484
00:39:40,640 --> 00:39:44,380
Many observers felt the success in this new era

485
00:39:44,381 --> 00:39:46,740
would be won not by military might,

486
00:39:46,860 --> 00:39:50,100
but by education and economic competitiveness.

487
00:39:57,860 --> 00:40:00,100
As we face the new millennium,

488
00:40:00,540 --> 00:40:03,440
the importance of our oceans is becoming much more clear.

489
00:40:04,240 --> 00:40:06,620
We will either destroy the sea through ignorance,

490
00:40:06,880 --> 00:40:10,700
or save it, and ourselves, with the kinds of knowledge now unfolding.

491
00:40:12,160 --> 00:40:15,600
The discovery of the mineral-laden hydrothermal vents

492
00:40:15,601 --> 00:40:20,640
and superheated microbes has led to new breakthroughs throughout the scientific world.

493
00:40:21,520 --> 00:40:26,520
The ocean depths hold many other unknown and undreamed of medical possibilities.

494
00:40:27,040 --> 00:40:29,860
Just on the surface, the probability is certainly large

495
00:40:29,861 --> 00:40:36,980
that there are enzymes, microbes, organisms, compounds in that vast ecosystem

496
00:40:36,981 --> 00:40:42,220
that we don't yet know, we haven't yet seen, much less studied,

497
00:40:42,221 --> 00:40:47,660
that could be of potential pharmaceutical or medicinal purpose.

498
00:40:48,000 --> 00:40:54,560
If we upset the ocean's delicate balance with pollution, overfishing, and nuclear and chemical wastes,

499
00:40:55,040 --> 00:41:01,440
we could drive species to extinction and destroy the life-giving possibilities of the sea.

500
00:41:01,900 --> 00:41:08,260
The abyss also holds archaeological treasures and the keys to ancient civilizations.

501
00:41:09,100 --> 00:41:14,400
In many respects, the deep is the greatest and perhaps the deadliest museum that we have on Earth.

502
00:41:14,680 --> 00:41:19,080
Because of this icy cold temperature, many things are well preserved.

503
00:41:19,540 --> 00:41:22,900
There are people who think that there are whole galleons, lost galleons,

504
00:41:23,240 --> 00:41:27,460
from 500 years ago that are down there, that are virtually intact.

505
00:41:28,220 --> 00:41:30,720
The Cold War has produced this explosion of technology,

506
00:41:31,080 --> 00:41:37,220
but the ugly legacy of the Cold War is all this nuclear garbage and refuse and warheads that are laying down there.

507
00:41:38,260 --> 00:41:47,400
If done correctly, the excavation of this archive could expand our knowledge and open the door to long lost worlds.

508
00:41:49,480 --> 00:41:56,080
Extensive experimentation is also currently underway on using the ocean as an energy source.

509
00:41:57,660 --> 00:42:01,420
Power derived from the differences in seawater temperature,

510
00:42:02,280 --> 00:42:04,480
from the changing movements of the tides,

511
00:42:04,481 --> 00:42:12,080
and from hydrothermal vents are all possible non-polluting energy sources of the future.

512
00:42:16,340 --> 00:42:19,460
The ocean is the key to our climate and weather.

513
00:42:20,200 --> 00:42:22,600
We now know that in a long chain reaction,

514
00:42:23,040 --> 00:42:27,360
the disruption of the ocean's currents, temperature or life forms in one area

515
00:42:27,361 --> 00:42:32,060
can set off a series of storms and droughts on the other side of the world.

516
00:42:34,480 --> 00:42:41,480
Unlocking the interconnected mysteries of the sea is critical to understanding the world's climate.

517
00:42:41,481 --> 00:42:48,480
Pollution, national security and fishing and mining rights are all oceanic issues

518
00:42:48,481 --> 00:42:53,480
that could lead to explosive international concerns in the coming decades.

519
00:42:53,481 --> 00:43:00,480
There are huge amounts of potential riches that can be extracted in the decades and the centuries ahead.

520
00:43:00,481 --> 00:43:01,330
countries ahead.

521
00:43:01,331 --> 00:43:05,100
Where are we going to divide the lines between various sovereign states

522
00:43:05,101 --> 00:43:06,995
that are making claims on the ocean?

523
00:43:06,996 --> 00:43:10,100
Undoubtedly in the future there are going to be wars fought

524
00:43:10,101 --> 00:43:11,272
over this wealth.

525
00:43:11,273 --> 00:43:16,100
The nations of the world face a profound challenge in the years ahead.

526
00:43:16,101 --> 00:43:22,100
The fate of the oceans and our interlocking ecosystem may depend on the decisions we make

527
00:43:22,101 --> 00:43:23,956
in the decades to come.

528
00:43:23,957 --> 00:43:28,960
But along with this burden comes extraordinary opportunity and

529
00:43:28,961 --> 00:43:34,920
the wondrous discovery of a whole new world.

530
00:43:34,921 --> 00:43:36,860
Billions of years ago,

531
00:43:36,861 --> 00:43:44,040
man's ancestors emerged from the oceans to begin their evolutionary path on land.

532
00:43:44,041 --> 00:43:47,880
The world of tomorrow may find man retracing that journey

533
00:43:47,881 --> 00:43:49,860
and returning once again

534
00:43:49,861 --> 00:43:51,380
to his home

535
00:43:51,381 --> 00:43:56,380
in the sea.

